Subject: [PATCH] ubus: bound the neighbour report copy in ubus_get_rrm_cb

ubus_get_rrm_cb() copies hostapd's "rrm_nr_get_own" reply into
hostapd_sock_entry::neighbor_report with a plain strcpy(). That buffer is
NEIGHBOR_REPORT_LEN + 1 (201) bytes and is followed in the same struct by
the ubus_subscriber, so a reply longer than 200 hex characters runs into
the subscriber's avl_node -- the exact node libubus inserted into
ctx->objects.

The QSDK hostapd on this target returns one report for every BSS of the
phy; with three radios it is 304 hex characters. The overflow turns the
subscribed object's avl_node (list.next, parent, left, right, key) into
ASCII hex digits, and the next ubus object insertion walks those pointers
and dies with SIGSEGV inside avl_insert() -- one core every few seconds,
each crash additionally stalling for the vendor core-upload retry.

Copy at most NEIGHBOR_REPORT_LEN characters and terminate explicitly,
mirroring what msghandler.c already does for the same field, and warn when
the report does not fit. Also skip the copy when the request no longer
belongs to a known socket (entry == NULL) and when the attribute is not a
string, both of which dereference NULL today.

--- a/src/utils/ubus.c	2026-11-14 02:13:57.381055387 +0000
+++ b/src/utils/ubus.c	2026-11-14 02:14:18.352021316 +0000
@@ -968,9 +968,31 @@
      {
          if(i==2)
          {
-            char* neighborreport = blobmsg_get_string(attr);
-            strcpy(entry->neighbor_report,neighborreport);
-            dawnlog_debug("Copied Neighborreport: %s,\n", entry->neighbor_report);
+            const char *neighborreport;
+            size_t nrl;
+
+            // The request may already have been sent when the interface was
+            // removed, in which case no hostapd_sock_entry owns req->peer.
+            if (!entry)
+                break;
+
+            neighborreport = blobmsg_get_string(attr);
+            if (!neighborreport)
+                break;
+
+            // neighbour_report below is NEIGHBOR_REPORT_LEN + 1 bytes and is
+            // followed in the same struct by the ubus_subscriber, so the copy
+            // must stay bounded even when hostapd returns more than the buffer
+            // holds: QSDK hostapd answers with one element per BSS of the phy
+            // and easily exceeds the length dawn was written for.
+            strncpy(entry->neighbor_report, neighborreport, NEIGHBOR_REPORT_LEN);
+            entry->neighbor_report[NEIGHBOR_REPORT_LEN] = '\0';
+
+            nrl = strlen(neighborreport);
+            if (nrl > NEIGHBOR_REPORT_LEN)
+                dawnlog_warning("Neighbor Report possibly truncated - hex length of %zu\n", nrl);
+            else
+                dawnlog_debug("Copied Neighborreport: %s,\n", entry->neighbor_report);
          }
          i++;
      }
--- a/src/include/datastorage.h	2026-11-14 02:13:57.384040032 +0000
+++ b/src/include/datastorage.h	2026-11-14 02:13:57.385553686 +0000
@@ -201,7 +201,12 @@
 
 // ---------------- Defines ----------------
 // FIXME: Is this enough of the NR to allow 802.11 operations to work?  Should we go for max possible length of 256 * 2 + 1
-#define NEIGHBOR_REPORT_LEN 200
+// A Neighbor Report element carries a one-byte length, so one element is at
+// most 257 bytes (514 hex chars), and hostapd returns one element per BSS it
+// serves. The QSDK hostapd on this target already answers with 304-330 hex
+// chars for a single interface; 200 overflowed both this buffer and the
+// ubus_subscriber that follows it in hostapd_sock_entry.
+#define NEIGHBOR_REPORT_LEN 1024
 /* Neighbor report string elements
  * [Elemen ID|1][LENGTH|1][BSSID|6][BSSID INFORMATION|4][Operating Class|1][Channel Number|1][PHY Type|1][Operational Subelements]
  * first two bytes are not stored
